WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.740
Film Courage: How many talented ones, truly
great writers would you say have MFA’s,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.839
even have a bachelor’s versus those that
maybe don’t?

00:00:08.839 --> 00:00:10.690
Do you think it’s really necessary?

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:14.980
David Willis: Gosh…I don’t know?

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:21.320
I know someone who just got a Master’s in
television writing from Loyola [University].

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:27.939
But I really don’t know everyone’s resumes
in these groups and what they’re into.

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:32.529
The nice thing about (I’ve noticed) people
who go to college and they get these screenwriting

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:37.540
degrees is when you’re young, you just don’t
have that much to write about, yet?

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.270
Because you haven’t…it’s like a lot
of comedians don’t get successful until

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:46.280
they are at least like 30 because they just
haven’t done that much yet.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.739
You just need time on the planet and as a
writer the more time you have on the planet,

00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:54.699
the more you have to draw from.

00:00:54.699 --> 00:00:58.350
So that’s the great thing about going to
college is that while you’re young and still

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:03.950
learning you can learn the craft and the technique
so that when you do have more things to write

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.240
about, you have the technique.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.280
Film Courage: You’ve got to get bitter first!

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.080
Probably around 35 is the time for this to
happen.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.650
David Willis: Well writers are frequently
(even when they’re successful) there’s

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.110
a bitterness.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.470
When writers get together they just love to
complain.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.660
If you don’t have an agent, you love to
complain about that you don’t have an agent.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.000
When you do have an agent you complain that
they are not sending out your stuff enough.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.870
Okay, when you sell a pilot you complain because
the pilot didn’t get made.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.799
When you get the pilot made, you complain
because it didn’t go to series.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:38.490
When you have a series you complain that it
only ran two years instead of six years.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.390
Okay and I guess if you are Chuck Lorre you
complain that you only have three series on

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:43.820
the air instead of six.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.250
I mean writers will always find a reason to
complain.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.420
We’re creative that way.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.200
Film Courage: Right, but they voice what most
people are already thinking, but maybe they

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.840
just don’t want to say it.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.230
So those that are embittered enough to say
it, we’re like “Yes!

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.600
We won’t say that but we want to
hear it.”

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.250
David Willis: Well, that’s a writer’s
job to voice things that people can’t say.

